# Dependencies

Whether kelpie merges the bumps bots open (Dependabot), and what it does with one that breaks. #855

## Settings

- `[app.dogs.kelpie.dependencies]`, `#[serde(default)]`, `deny_unknown_fields`: an absent table is `pick = "off"`. `src/settings/dependencies.rs`
- `pick`: `off` (default; every bot's pull request is left alone) or `on`.
- `bots`: logins whose pull requests are bumps, `["dependabot"]` when absent. Compared ignoring case and a `[bot]` suffix. Not the contributions' bot rule: a bump never waits on the maintainer's labels.
- `on_red`: `close` (default) or `leave`. `fix` is not built.
- `web`: paths that make a bump a web bump. A path ending `/` is a folder, any other is a file. Empty means no bump is one.
- `wait_hours` (24): above 0. How long a head's checks may stay pending, or never start, before the bump is red.
- `reread` keeps the running table like the other project tables, so a change waits for the next start. `src/runner/reread.rs`

## Detection

- A bump is an open pull request, not a draft, from a branch of the repo itself, whose author is a listed bot. `is_bump`, `src/runner/dependencies.rs`. A bump takes no slot, no ruling and no model call.
- `ProjectState.bumps` holds `Bump { pull_request, author, branch, head, seen, rebase_asked, e2e, standing, red }`, oldest first. A new head replaces `head` and `seen` and clears `e2e`; `rebase_asked` names the head last asked, so the new head is asked afresh. `src/state/bumps.rs`
- `Runner::follow_dependencies` runs once a `BOARD_POLL` after `follow_contributions`, off the same listing. A bump that left the board is dropped, unless kelpie had decided to close it and still owes its issue; that record is kept whether the pull request left the board, became a draft or stopped being a listed bot's. One closed by someone else before kelpie's comment or close skips them and gets the issue; one merged by hand gets none, and a pull request that cannot be read waits. `pick = "off"` forgets all.

## Proving a head

- Behind or conflicting with `origin/main` (`worktree::base_of`): `@<author> rebase` (the login without a `[bot]` suffix, which a mention does not resolve) is posted once per head (`rebase_asked`), as the maintainer's `gh` login, and nothing else is proved on that head. A conflicting bump is asked the same way: Dependabot decides whether it can.
- On `main`: wait `CHECKS_SETTLE` (120 s) from `seen`, as the gate does, then read the checks. `Passed` and `Skipped` are green. Failed is red.
- Pending or absent checks are red once `wait_hours` have passed since `seen`.
- A web bump (`changed_paths` of the head against `origin/main` meets `web`) also needs a proof of its head: the local browser where the project has one, the e2e host where it has that, and both must pass when it has both. `src/runner/flight/proving.rs`
- No run starts while the runner drains, and a run in flight shows in the drain's `calls` as role `e2e`, so an upgrade or pause waits for it. `src/runner/flight/calls.rs`
- The proof is one thread at a time: check the head out at `worktrees/bump-<n>`, run the browser proof if planned, ask the dog (`E2eRunner`, `lease e2e`'s ask), remove the checkout, send the verdict as news. The dog finds the project from that folder. `src/adapters/e2e_runner.rs`
- Outcomes (`Ended`, `src/lease/cli/e2e.rs`, not the exit code, since the command's own code can equal the dog's): the command exiting 0 passes and is saved; exiting anything else is red and saved with the end of its output; the dog saying there is no host leaves the bump unmerged and `status` says so, and the dog is not asked again for that head until the runner restarts; the dog unreached or gone, or refusing the ask, is no run and is asked again at the next poll.
- The project has the browser when `browser.enabled`, this is macOS, the tools are installed and `main` has a launch file with a configuration (#861). Without a launch file the e2e host alone proves it; a launch file that cannot be read or a port that cannot be had leaves the bump open, `status` saying why, and is asked again at the next poll. `Runner::browser_proof`, `src/runner/dev_server/bump.rs`
- The browser proof starts that dev server in the bump's checkout, in a sandbox that writes only the checkout and `builds/bump-<n>`, reaches only `browser.domains` and cannot read credentials, and opens its port to the browser only once it answers. The browser is headless whatever `headed` says, behind the same bridge, filter and proxy as a worker's (`PlaywrightPages`, `src/adapters/pages.rs`). Kelpie speaks MCP to its server with no model (`src/adapters/bridge/client.rs`, a recorded session in `fixtures/playwright-mcp-session.jsonl`).
- Each of `browser.routes` is opened at `http://localhost:<port><route>`. A route passes when the page loads, no request to the dev server's origin answers 5xx, and the console logs no error. A failed route is red like a failed e2e run (the browser proof ends the head's proof, so no e2e run follows), and the failure names the route and the status, never a URL or a path: ``route `/` had a request answer 503``, ``route `/` logged 2 console errors``, ``route `/` did not load``. It is posted and not saved apart from `Red`.
- A browser proof is not saved when it passes: the e2e run follows in the same thread, and the pass is saved in `Bump.e2e` when that ends (a host that is not there counts as a pass when the browser proof passed). A restart repeats both. A dev server that does not answer, or cannot start, leaves the bump open and is not tried again for that head until the runner restarts. A browser that cannot be driven, or fewer pages than asked, is tried again at the next poll.
- A proved bump is merged with `Forge::merge` (a merge commit, `--match-head-commit`). Merges come one at a time without a queue: the next bump is behind `main` and is asked to rebase.

## Red

- Under `close`, steps are saved one at a time in `Red.step`, so a restart repeats none: comment naming each failed check (the e2e output tail is not posted), close the pull request (`Forge::close_pull_request`), then open an issue labelled `needs-triage` and `dependencies`. A refused step is tried again at the next poll.
- Under `leave` the reason is logged once and the bump stays open. A new head gives it another chance.
- Out of scope: `on_red = "fix"`.

## Log and status

- One log line each: rebase ask, e2e run (passed, failed), merge, red, close, issue filed. `bump #<n>: ...`
- `status` lists `bumps` with the pull request, author, head and standing, left out when empty.
